 #:seon.task{:name "adoption-readopts-what-the-jvm-loaded"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A move to HEAD spends 6.4-9.9 s adopting after readiness (development-source-refresh! 5,171 ms, adopt-entities! 2,761 ms) re-adopting what the fresh JVM already loaded; boot's store->branch layer is 27-32 s whenever the commit touches analyzer reach (full-source-refresh! :all: lint ~8 s, analysis ~8 s, three transactions ~10 s); today's nuke was ready in 43 s. Both are over the 10-second start law. 2026-10-07 start --head onto 108dd1c3e: ready 51.0 s, :seon.source/current 44.5 s publishing 132 of 847 changed inputs (.clj-kondo/config.edn among them, which reselects every source), then a post-readiness adoption of 20.8 s (refresh-source! -> development-source-refresh! 20.5 s, adopt-entities! 15.8 s, one 15.0 s transact! with write-report validation 4.5 s, published-changes 3.8 s). An unchanged restart selects 0 paths in 79 ms, so the publication is catch-up; the re-adoption is per move."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/adopt-entities! seon.cluster/development-source-refresh! seon.cluster/full-source-refresh!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/inv-boot-operator-opus-2026-09-26.md"
                         "docs/research/agent-platform/perf-profile-2026-09-26.md"
                         "docs/seon/issues/move-to-head-takes-a-minute-or-more-not-seconds.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "record-jvm-evaluations-on-shared-path"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A JVM-mode MCP evaluation is evaluated by the prepl and projected by mcp-project, but never recorded: it has no evaluation record, no settlement window and so no fault observation, unlike an SCI run. The JVM evaluator should be a stage value supplied to the one read-evaluate-record-print path (no custody), so its value, its error in the one shape and its window's faults are the same saved observation MCP and the context show."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/mcp-project seon.cluster/mcp-valf}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/error-delivery-audit-2026-09-26.md"
                         "docs/research/agent-platform/landing/err-s4-observation-opus-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-mcp-value-is-a-converted-tree"
             :kind :function
             :intent "The MCP :seon.dev.mcp/value is the selected print tree converted back to data (admit/semantic-value) with elision maps inline, not the shared AI text the one-render-path design names (its 3h item 9); a stored backing also reads a sorted map back in hash order."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/mcp-project}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/fix-floor-review-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-missed-quiescence-keeps-the-route-listener"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A stop that misses armer quiescence keeps the route listener: a wedged proc cannot run its own stop transition. Recommended option 2 queues flow/stop even on a miss (about 15 lines in disarm-agents!)."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/disarm-agents!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/inv-lifecycle-storage-opus-2026-09-26.md"
                         "docs/seon/issues/a-cluster-stop-that-misses-armer-quiescence-keeps-the-route-listener.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "adoption-write-costs-sixteen-seconds"
             :kind :function
             :intent "start --head spends 15.9 s in the adoption write, mostly seon.db with-declarations x18,794; over the one-second rule. Cost must follow the changed declarations."
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/fix-dial-retirement-adoption-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "boot-publication-phases-are-invisible"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A boot resume publication runs before arm-host-program!, so no profile cell observes it and refresh-source!'s explain-slow says nothing; *source-progress!* is bound nowhere (report-source-progress! is a no-op outside tests), so its phase lines vanish. The 132 s store->branch layer of the e405f51be boot could only be split by replaying the publication on a scratch branch (tmp/perf-boot-opus/replay.clj). Carry the boot publication's phase milliseconds in its result and the instance's readiness. Still true 2026-10-07: the 44.5 s :seon.source/current layer of the 108dd1c3e boot has no profile cell; seon.log has two lines for the whole boot."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/refresh-source! seon.cluster/populate-source!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/perf-boot-opus-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-test-adoption-moves-the-jvms-loaded-identity"
             :kind :function
             :intent "seon.sci.eval/loaded-identity is one process-wide atom, and call-with-program-change (from development-source-refresh!) advances it to the commit the adopting store published. A test that boots a cluster on its own root and store and adopts there (seon.adoption-contract-freshness-test calls refresh-source! with a development cluster) therefore renames the JVM's loaded program after a commit of another store, and later test requests' seon.test/arm-first-loads! open that commit through seon.cluster.source/database. That is JVM-global test state (CLAUDE.md). Unconfirmed instance: shrink-test saw requests at 18:20:59 and 18:24:08 CST open 6ab855c9 (default's 17:31 publication) through that path although the cluster record named 6ab86150; the note attributes them to pid 50733, which started only at 18:26:24 CST, so they ran on the JVM before it. Key the loaded identity by the store whose program the JVM loaded, and never let an adoption on another store move it."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/development-source-refresh! seon.sci.eval/loaded-identity seon.test/arm-first-loads!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/perf-boot-opus-2026-09-26.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "mcp-reads-the-jvm-wide-instance-roster"
             :kind :function
             :intent "mcp-instance reads seon.operator.runtime/running-instances directly with no way to pass an instance, so mcp-test fixtures swap! the JVM-wide roster 16 times (JVM-global test state) and one test builds its context with env/carry-state {} and with-redefs over config/effective. Pass the instance through the mcp-* functions (mcp-valf, mcp-get-value, mcp-effective, runtime observation) and convert the fixtures to injected instances."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/mcp-get-value}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/red-triage-3-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "mcp-reply-without-a-database-prints-unbounded"
             :kind :function
             :intent "In JVM mode for a cluster with no connection (before the store layer is up, or a cluster name this JVM does not host), mcp-project has no database for render.value/prepare, whose unit requires :seon.db/db; the refusal is attached and the value falls back to plain printing with the MCP profile's budget unapplied. AGENTS: nothing prints unbounded. The fallback prints through the floor's bounded path (bounded-text), never plain Clojure printing of the whole value (fix-c, 4c2ac5774)."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/mcp-project seon.render.value/prepare}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/red-triage-3-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "incremental-publication-test-redefines-process-wide-vars"
             :kind :function
             :intent "seon.incremental-publication-test/non-program-inputs-have-their-own-publication-owner points publication at a fixture checkout with with-redefs-fn over clojure.java.io/resource and seon.cluster/publication-roots. Both are Var roots, so every thread of the shared JVM (default's agents, other test members) reads the fixture's schemas and roots while it runs. Give refresh-source! a source seam (publication roots and the schema resource directory passed as values) so the test hands its checkout, or declare the test isolated until then."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/refresh-source! seon.cluster/publication-roots}
             :evidence #{"test/seon/incremental_publication_test.clj"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "schema-adoption-refuses-instead-of-dropping-an-edn-encoded-value"
             :kind :function
             :intent "invalid-value-retraction decodes each stored value with seon.schema.datahike/decode-attribute-value-in, which for an EDN-encoded attribute validates the decoded value against the attribute's NEW form and throws ::schema-invalid (datahike.clj:328-331). So a narrowed EDN-encoded attribute makes the whole adoption throw instead of retracting only the refused values, and an EDN-encoded cardinality-many attribute would validate one member against its whole [:vector ...] form. No installed many attribute is EDN-encoded today (117 probed on default, 2026-09-27), so the many case is latent. Decode without the slot validation here (the retraction validates), or share the writer's attribute plan (db.clj write-attribute-plan)."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/invalid-value-retraction seon.schema.datahike/decode-attribute-value-in}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/register-anywhere-diagnosis-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "runtime-status-throws-on-a-boot-window-read"
             :kind :function
             :intent "At 18:01:08Z 2026-09-27, 19 s into pid 75447, runtime_status threw open-faults' contract refusal ('expected must be an immutable Datahike database value, got a map') instead of answering: mcp-runtime-observation (cluster.clj ~620-624) calls db/db several times and passes its declared :seon.db/invalid-read-error map on (cause unverified). Read one database value; an error value makes problems unavailable (:seon.dev.mcp/problems-unavailable) and health :unknown. Done when a regression whose database read answers an error value gets an observation with health :unknown, never a throw."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/mcp-runtime-observation}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/stability-sweep-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "mcp-get-value-admits-a-negative-offset"
             :kind :function
             :intent "seon.cluster/mcp-get-value declares its offset as :int, so -1 passes its contract and fails one call later inside seon.render.walk/continue (\"expected ... an integer at least 0, got -1\"). Found by the install gate's auto-check (seed from the repl-dogfood run, 2026-09-27). The producer's contract should say [:int {:min 0}] so the refusal names mcp-get-value's own argument."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/mcp-get-value seon.render.walk/continue}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/repl-dogfood-2026-09-27.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "schema-convergence-sees-order-the-store-does-not-keep"
             :kind :function
             :intent "schema-entity-changes reports :seon.agent/agent, :seon.issue/issue and :seon.config.ai/thinking as unconverged on a fresh member branch, so every open re-transacts them: :seon.render/units is stored as a cardinality-many set (read back sorted) while the desired entity holds the declared vector, and store-comparable-value compares order since dd5dcaff7 (vector storage S9). cluster-test schema-entity-convergence-uses-the-stores-own-semantics is red (probed on default 18:07 local 2026-09-27). Compare a set-stored attribute as a set; order only where storage keeps it (a vector-cell attribute)."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/schema-entity-changes seon.cluster/schema-entity-converged?}
             :evidence #{"test/seon/cluster_test.clj"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "publication-tests-cost-35-seconds"
             :kind :function
             :intent "cluster.source-test flat-scratch-write-refusal-retires-the-candidate (35.6 s), incremental-publication-does-not-change-an-existing-cluster (40.0 s) and publication-hands-its-commit-projection-unstamped (35.7 s) pass but exceed their 5 s bound (r8 at 7533e982d); the long work-on-an-acquired-branch test then had 1.5 s of its request left. reconcile-test apply-then-reapply-converges-over-generated-populations took 35.3 s. Each over ten seconds needs its responsible armed functions named and fixed or owner authorization."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.source/publish! seon.reconcile/reconcile!}
             :evidence #{"test/seon/cluster/source_test.clj" "test/seon/reconcile_test.clj"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "schema-form-keys-re-derive-files-whose-entities-do-not-change"
             :kind :function
             :intent "A start onto 0f92ebdbc (34 changed files: 25 schema resources from e941b7845, repl.clj, fn.clj, 3 tests) was ready in 20.97 s against 5.97 s on an unchanged program: source current 17.3 s. The publication changed 106 derivation keys; 97 of those files had unchanged bytes and their re-derived entities asserted NOTHING but the new :seon.fn.file/derivation-digest (194 of the transaction's 2,145 datoms, zero other attributes; current-src tx 536870951, measured 2026-09-28). Re-analysis costs about 80 ms per file (106 files: 8.3 s armed, seon.fn.analyzer/analyze 4.7 s of it, invoke-kondo 2.3 s), so about 7.6 s of that boot re-derived identical entities. The key (derivation-keys) digests the whole form of every schema key a file's keywords and contracts name, closed over schema dependencies, so an edit that only drops render pairs from 82 error schemas re-keys every file whose contracts name one. Also, a publication whose keys are stale analyzes the changed files twice: full-source-refresh! analyzes `changed`, finds `stale`, then recurses and analyzes changed plus stale again. Done when a schema edit re-derives only files whose derived entities read what changed (key on what the derivation actually reads from the forms, or cut off early when the re-derived rows equal the stored ones before the rest of the publication), the changed files are analyzed once, and the same probe shows the boot source layer proportional to files whose entities change."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/derivation-keys seon.cluster/full-source-refresh! seon.cluster/derivation-schema-keys}
             :evidence #{"tmp/waste-profile/evidence.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "retiring-an-attribute-with-stored-component-data-refuses-adoption"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Removing :seon.maintenance.result/collect-branches (a component vector with no reader; its {:seon.store/branch :seon.source/commit-id} elements broke boot, fixed by 919be10c5) cannot adopt on default: the cluster population transaction refuses 'Complete component validation refused: vector-element-swept' (entity 106090, cell 106091) because the overnight collection receipt still holds the vector. Retiring a schema attribute must retract its stored values in the same population transaction (as drop-retired-families! does for retired identity families), not refuse. Done when that removal adopts on the live store and the receipt stops storing heads."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster/populate-source! seon.maintenance/project-collect-result}}
 #:seon.task{:name "reset-leaves-old-agents-branches-and-connections"
             :kind :function
             :intent "After `bin/seon reset default --force` the roster still held 16 :agent-* branches whose agents no longer existed on the reforked :cluster-default, each with an open connection in the JVM, so seon.cluster.registry/retire-branch! refused them ('still has a connection in this process'); the leaked hosts' loaded indexes held 4.6 GB of live heap. Reset must stop every hosted agent of the old cluster and release its connection, so boot's retire-unclaimed! retires the branches. Proof: a test that hosts agents on branches, stops the cluster's hosts and asserts no connection remains open for their branches."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.registry/retire-branch! seon.cluster.host/release-children!}}]
